      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I strongly recommend arguing that the unchecked expansion of executive privilege represents a dangerous erosion of democratic checks and balances rather than a necessary protection for the presidency. Your strengths would lie in demonstrating that the Framers' system of \"separation of powers\" does not grant the President a absolute right to withhold information from the legislative branch to avoid legislative accountability. By asserting that the \"sine qua non\" of the Constitution is the ability of one branch to check the others, your team can frame the issue as one of preventing \"imperial presidency\" tendencies, which resonates powerfully in modern governance debates.\n\nAdditionally, you should emphasize that legitimate national security concerns do not justify a complete exemption from congressional oversight, especially when intelligence is not the decisive factor in high-stakes policy. You can argue that the proper balance is achieved not by absolute privilege, but by a tailored deliberation process where the executive can justify the need for confidentiality while still maintaining the legislature's right to deliberate on policy impacts.\n\n**Stronger Legal Position Summary:**\nThe argument favoring **congressional oversight over broad executive privilege** is legally stronger and more persuasive for a modern moot court because it aligns with the \"modifier clause\" (logic of the \"one branch check the",
